WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.626 --> 00:00:13.180
KELLY STIRMAN: Hello, and
welcome to "Helping You

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:19.140
Realize the Full Potential of
PostgreSQL with Google Cloud."

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.000
my name is Kelly
Stirman, and I'm

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.020
a Director of Product Management
in the Databases group.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.660
Later in this presentation,
we'll hear from Phil and Tim

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:33.650
from Wayfair on how they're
using Postgres on Google Cloud.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.450
In this session,
we're going to discuss

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.660
why so many of our customers
are choosing Postgres to build

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.360
new operational
systems and what we're

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.270
doing to help address
some of their challenges.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.300
The team from Wayfair will talk
about their work with Postgres

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.540
on Google Cloud.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.230
So let's get started
and start by discussing

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.870
exactly what Postgres is and a
brief overview of its history.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.670
Postgres is a modern open-source
relational database, originally

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:11.040
developed at UC Berkeley, and
its first release was in 1996.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.470
While commonly used to
manage relational data,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.510
it supports a more comprehensive
set of data models,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.700
like JSON, Key Value,
XML, geospatial,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.720
and many more capabilities
through its powerful extension

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:27.710
model.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.140
This rich data
model support means

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:35.570
that Postgres is used in a wide
range of database use cases.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.430
Databases created
with Postgres are

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.250
used in every conceivable
type of application,

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.100
from the very simplest
single-user database

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.510
app to demanding enterprise
applications supporting

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.760
tens of thousands of
users running thousands

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.800
of transactions a second.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.530
It's found on the simplest
single-board computers

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.480
to the largest Linux
servers with hundreds

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.270
of cores and
terabytes of memory,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.170
managing petabytes of data.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.260
So why have so many
of our customers

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.020
adopted Postgres as
a database of choice?

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.350
Well, organizations have
started looking for alternatives

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.950
to the commercial database
they have historically used.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.770
They've done this as a means
of reducing the perceived

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.570
punitive cost and
the aggressive sales

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:23.500
practices that have somewhat
soured their experiences.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.720
But given the number of
open-source databases

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.620
that are available, why
should you consider Postgres?

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.690
Well, Postgres has a
number of attributes that

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.700
make that decision simpler.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.670
It provides a similar
and, in some cases,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.760
better set of functionality
than the expensive closed-source

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.380
alternatives.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:44.140
If you're looking for a database
that supports parallel query,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.580
partition tables, stored
logic, replication, change data

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:53.890
capture, rich data models, and
so on, all in a single product,

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.980
then look no further
than Postgres.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.740
On top of this
impressive functionality,

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.370
Postgres has demonstrated
itself in demanding customer

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.680
environments time
and time again,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.300
giving others the
confidence to use it

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.250
in their own applications.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.160
All this, and a friendly
and engaging community

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.260
with a rich set of
tools and utilities,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:19.780
has made Postgres an easy choice
for many of our customers.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.690
But perhaps the most
important aspect of Postgres

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.050
is its role as an open API.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.810
While SQL is commonly
considered the language of data,

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.360
and is indeed responsible
for the ongoing success

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.110
of relational databases,
each relational database

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.350
has its own implementation
with functions and operators

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.090
that behave slightly
differently,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.930
and more substantial differences
in programmatic APIs.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.600
What Postgres has the
potential to deliver

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.930
is a common SQL layer
that is consistent,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:53.530
regardless of the database
engine that underpins it.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.170
Postgres-- certainly,
its SQL layer--

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:00.430
could lead to a standardization
of databases in the same way

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.970
we've seen Linux change the
enterprise operating system

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:04.470
world.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.170
Cloud SQL for Postgres is part
of Google Cloud's fully managed

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.580
database portfolio.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:16.269
It provides fully managed,
reliable database instances

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:18.370
with features that
make databases

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.070
easy to set up,
back up, restore,

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.960
and provide high availability.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.630
It's the same
version of Postgres

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.480
that you can download from
the community website,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.490
with the ability to
choose between the version

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.800
of the database that
suits your requirements.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.650
Google takes care of the
heavy lifting on your behalf,

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.480
patching and securing
your databases

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.970
with very little downtime.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.270
Cloud SQL aims to provide
the best Postgres experience

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.270
anywhere in the cloud.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.890
So given the many
options you have

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.630
when deciding on where to
run your Postgres databases,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.305
why should you
choose Google Cloud?

00:04:52.305 --> 00:04:54.430
Well, Google understands
the importance of the role

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.160
that open source plays
in the enterprise

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.680
and is committed to provide
the best experience for users

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.210
of Postgres.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.952
We particularly
understand the importance

00:05:02.952 --> 00:05:04.660
that Postgres will
play as a SQL standard

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.320
for databases in the future.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.020
We offer a one-of-a-kind
platform to run your Postgres

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.890
databases, using the same
infrastructure that runs many

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:17.130
of the Google applications
you use daily.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.800
You get access to fast
and scalable compute

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.320
and high-performance,
low-latency networks

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.820
with a global reach.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.150
Google continues to invest
in its own dedicated

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:30.750
global network backbone
and standing up

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.720
data centers around
the world to ensure

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.760
you have the fastest and most
secure access to your data.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.240
We will continue to offer the
best experience for developers,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.080
whether you're building out
your own VM-based applications

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.690
or building them on
top of Kubernetes.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.020
Google Cloud offers modern
APIs and frameworks,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.290
whether you plan to
use Postgres to build

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.560
modern microservices
or more traditional

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.380
monolithic applications.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.600
We understand developers
and are committed

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.610
to making your teams as
productive as possible.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.210
We've created integrations
and management tools

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.420
to simplify how you make
the most of your data.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.670
And we provide
rich and open APIs

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.340
to protect your
application investments.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.660
We've been hard at work
adding important features

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.030
to Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:29.380
Recently, we added support for
logical replication and IAM

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.140
authentication, making
Cloud SQL easier

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:37.100
to integrate with your
core enterprise systems.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.810
Cloud SQL for Postgres now
supports over 140 flags

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.330
and 44 extensions.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.810
We have tuned the operating
systems on our Cloud SQL

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.810
instances for
database workloads.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.940
Google Cloud was
the first to support

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:55.250
Postgres 13 in the cloud.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:58.700
And in just a moment, we'll
talk about Cloud SQL insights

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.220
and cost recommendations
via Active Assist.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:08.200
Our database migration service
makes migration simple, safe,

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.900
and secure.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.870
DMS is serverless, so there's
nothing for you to provision

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.190
or manage.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.640
DMS will do all that
heavy lifting for you.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:21.690
More than 85% of DMS
migrations are underway

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.190
in less than an hour.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.810
And a majority of
these migrations

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.680
are coming from other clouds.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.410
Observability is critical
for managing your database

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:32.340
deployments.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:34.410
Recently, we launched
SQL Insights,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.410
giving you best-in-class
database observability using

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.120
open standards.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.220
SQL Insights helps
your developer teams

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.793
to understand
performance issues,

00:07:42.793 --> 00:07:44.460
whether you're building
a monolithic app

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.920
or using microservices.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.960
And now we provide
cost recommendations

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.320
through Active Assist, helping
you right-size your resources

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.560
and optimize your spend,
potentially saving you

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.860
thousands of dollars a month.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.150
So thus far, we've been talking
about Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.980
Now I'd like to spend
a few minutes talking

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:08.780
about Cloud Spanner and some
work we're doing with Postgres.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.460
Cloud Spanner is the
relational database

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.060
that banks, game studios,
retailers and organizations,

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.460
and many other industries
trust with their most

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.810
demanding workloads.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.060
It's the same database
that powers Google's

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:22.280
most recognizable products.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:25.400
Spanner offers global
consistency and five nines

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.710
of availability at
almost any scale,

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:32.150
without painful sharding or
re-architecture as you grow.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.669
Spanner's new
lower cost of entry

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:38.000
now makes realizing these
benefits even easier.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.429
You can start small and
be assured that Spanner

00:08:40.429 --> 00:08:43.679
will scale to meet your needs.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:47.280
Even with Spanner and all of
Google Cloud's unique benefits,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.710
our customers want options.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.770
Spanner isn't the only managed
database service out there.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.045
We want our customers
to feel confident

00:08:55.045 --> 00:08:56.670
that they'll be able
to move their data

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.420
and applications elsewhere
without a disruptive rewrite.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:03.510
The PostgreSQL interface
for Cloud Spanner

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.200
that we announced earlier today
will provide the scalability

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.430
and reliability of Spanner
that enterprises trust, along

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.760
with the familiarity and
portability of Postgres

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:16.275
that developers love.

00:09:16.275 --> 00:09:18.900
We're making it so you can bring
your existing skills and tools

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.540
around Postgres
to take advantage

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.130
of the unique
capabilities Spanner

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.920
has to offer in terms of
availability and scalability.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.185
So, to sum it up,
Postgres is becoming

00:09:32.185 --> 00:09:33.560
the relational
database of choice

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.800
for the enterprise
for many good reasons.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.620
We believe Google Cloud
offers the best place

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.590
to run your Postgres databases.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:48.730
And we're seeing a trend to
make Postgres's SQL layer

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:54.070
act as an open API for
applications and deployments.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:58.870
Now, I'd like to hand
things over to our friends

00:09:58.870 --> 00:09:59.980
from Wayfair.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.280
Tim and Phil,
please take it away.

