00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:24.260
In modern, fast-paced
technological environments,

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.210
time to market is critical.

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:29.390
And PostgreSQL on
Google's Cloud SQL

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:32.870
allows us to minimize the
effort of preparing our products

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.480
and applications
to serve production

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.440
workloads, while significantly
reducing the overall cost

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:40.940
of the solution.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:45.130
We've discovered that supporting
similar workloads on Cloud SQL

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:49.580
and PostgreSQL is anywhere
from four to eight times

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:52.340
cheaper than a comparable
commercial database

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.860
engine provisioned
on Compute Engine,

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.020
after accounting
for licensing costs

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:59.330
and depending on the
redundancy and distribution

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:02.820
level of the
deployment, of course.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.120
On top of that
difference, with Cloud SQL

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.610
being a completely
managed service,

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.680
there are additional
cost savings

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.900
associated with
dramatic reduction

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:15.830
in operating costs compared
to deploying on premises

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:18.410
or in an unmanaged fashion.

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:23.300
This makes PostgreSQL a
perfect, cost-effective solution

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.600
for the majority of online
relational data storage

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.040
needs in Wayfair.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.010
However, for some
distributed use cases

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:35.570
where consistent, safe
writes to the same data set

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:38.390
are happening from
multiple locations,

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:41.570
spanning countries and
sometimes continents,

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:44.360
a slightly different
solution is required.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.300
That's where Google
Cloud Spanner comes in,

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:50.450
a truly distributed
and truly consistent

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:53.450
solution for relational
database workloads that

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.850
require distribution.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:58.850
We're able to provision
distributed data stores

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:02.750
within minutes and with
almost zero operational effort

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:04.640
required from us.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.530
Thanks to Google's
TrueTime distributed clock,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:11.150
Spanner is capable of providing
the same consistency guarantees

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:15.530
as PostgreSQL at a minimal
added latency cost,

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:19.130
switching from writing and
storing data in a single data

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.320
center to persisting
the same data

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.350
points in a globally
distributed database.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:26.690
And having the data
available locally

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.720
for clients around
the world requires

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.580
next to zero development time.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:35.180
And with technologies
like PostgreSQL Interface

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:37.820
for Spanner, software
product journeys

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.940
can start with a local
PostgreSQL database

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:44.780
and shift towards Spanner
later in the lifecycle,

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:48.650
providing global availability
in an effortless fashion.

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.510
Back to Tim.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:53.370
TIM QUINNEY: Echoing what
both Phil and Kelly shared,

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.080
the value proposition
of Postgres

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:56.760
is highly resonant to us.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.830
It's open source
and cost efficient.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:00.660
It has tremendous
community support,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.720
with low barriers of
adoption for our users.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.570
Google's investment in database
products leveraging Postgres,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:10.260
from Cloud SQL to the new
Postgres interface in Spanner,

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.810
becomes increasingly
compelling for us,

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:16.170
because these products are
offered as managed services

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:18.570
and enable portability
to follow the lifecycle

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:20.400
of an application.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:22.860
I'm really excited about
this concept of portability.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:26.680
An application can start with
a simple Cloud SQL instance.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:28.935
Maybe it's a proof of concept
on a Friday afternoon.

00:16:28.935 --> 00:16:32.580
And before the team knows it,
it's a runtime-critical system.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.490
In due configuration, you
can enable scale read.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.700
If it grows to the occasion
needing global distribution,

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:40.860
it can be migrated
over to Spanner

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.770
without the need of a
significant re-architecture.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.800
This saves us from over
provisioning infrastructure

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.740
from perpetual rewrites
of applications

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:53.490
and from the significant
overhead of managing

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.170
a sharded database environment.

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:59.260
For these reasons, teams
such as our supply chain,

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.560
catalog engineering,
marketing technology,

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.319
are already leveraging
Postgres on Cloud SQL

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:05.829
for some of their systems.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:08.520
Additionally, Spanner is
quickly gaining traction,

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:10.920
their home services
and customs platform

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:13.200
teams recently adopting.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.270
As we look to the future,
we're excited to see

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.520
what kind of performance
and operational gains

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.380
can be achieved as we consider
moving other systems to Cloud

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:22.260
SQL and Cloud Spanner.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:24.540
One that comes to mind
that's particularly exciting

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:27.780
for us is our user profile
service, which is currently

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:29.460
on a sharded
database environment

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:30.780
across many servers.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:34.110
This is a prime candidate
for migration to Spanner

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.750
and core to our customer
shopping experience.

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.323
And now I'll hand
it back to Kelly.

00:17:39.323 --> 00:17:41.240
KELLY STIRMAN: Thanks,
Phil and Tim, for that.

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.910
And thanks to all of you
for joining us today.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:45.320
We appreciate it.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.460
Hope you have a great day.

